      {
        "review_id": 925420584,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r925420584-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Only for Hindus.",
        "text": "One of the holiest Hindu temples not only in Nepal, located approx. 5 km from the center of the capital. It is dedicated to the god Shiva. Situated on the bank of the Bagmati River, right next to the gates used for traditional cremations. The current temple building was built at the end of the 17th century. It has the form of a Nepalese pagoda with a gold-plated roof. Only Hindus are allowed to enter the temple. We start our visit here by buying a ticket at the tourist office. The ticket costs 1,000 rupees. On its back there is information about the regulations and rules for visiting this place. For tourists, the only option is to cross the Bagmati River to its eastern side by bridge. From the other bank you can watch the main temple, funeral ceremonies and cremations. You can also easily walk between the numerous chapels and take photos with the Sadhu \"saints\" posing [for a fee]. The complex has been on the UNESCO World Heritage List since 1979.",
